{"type":"rewrite","text":"A campus library is more than a place where students borrow books. It is also a place where they can build habits that help them study without constant instruction. For this reason, universities should treat the library as an important part of daily learning.\n\nThe library provides a quiet room for students who cannot concentrate in a crowded dormitory. Students can study there after class and compare several sources before forming an opinion. When they encounter an unfamiliar term, a librarian can show them how to search for reliable information instead of giving them a ready answer.\n\nThe library also provides workshops. A short session about planning a research project can help beginners divide a large task into manageable steps. However, workshops should remain optional, since students have different schedules and levels of experience.\n\nTo make these services useful, the university should ask students what they need and publish clear opening hours. With suitable support and enough freedom to choose their own methods, students can gradually become more responsible for their learning."}

