{"type":"rewrite","text":"Group study can be useful when students are learning a difficult subject, mainly because explaining ideas aloud reveals gaps in their understanding. However, simply sitting at the same table does not guarantee that everyone will learn.\n\nIn a well-organised session, each member prepares a question before the meeting. Each student has time to describe a problem, while the others listen and ask for clarification. Explaining a solution can make a concept easier to remember, because the speaker has to connect the individual steps and put them in order.\n\nHowever, group study also has its limits. One confident member may answer every question, leaving quieter students with little opportunity to think. A meeting can also turn into a social conversation if the group has no clear goal. These problems are especially serious when an examination is close and study time is limited.\n\nI would therefore combine group sessions with individual practice. Members could agree on a short agenda, take turns leading the discussion and finish by solving a new problem alone. This approach preserves the benefits of cooperation while allowing each student to check personal progress."}

